Fix the )LOAD timestamp printing single-digit date fields backwards.

)LOAD reports e.g. "SAVED 2026-70-30" for the 30th of July: the month is
padded on the wrong side. The workspace file itself is correct — it stores
month="7" — so only the report is wrong, and only for fields that need
padding, i.e. any single-digit month, day, hour, minute or second.

Cause: Command.cc puts the output stream into left-justified mode before
dispatching a command (Command.cc:241 and :379, `out << left << ...`), and
`left` is sticky. Archive.cc then formats the timestamp with setw(2) and
setfill('0'), which assumes the default right-justification, so the fill
lands after the digit instead of before it.

)SAVE is unaffected because it formats through
Workspace::get_v_Quad_TZ().print_timestamp() (Command.cc:1069) rather than
by hand.

Fix: ask for right-justification explicitly for the timestamp, and restore
left afterwards so later command output is unchanged. This one is not
libapl-specific — the standalone apl interpreter prints the same wrong date.

--- apl-2.0/src/Archive.cc
+++ apl-2.0/src/Archive.cc
@@ -1664,14 +1664,14 @@
 const char * tz_sign = (offset < 0) ? "" : "+";
    if (!silent)   out
         << "SAVED "
-        << setfill('0') << year        << "-"
+        << setfill('0') << right       << year        << "-"
         << setw(2)      << mon         << "-"
         << setw(2)      << day         << " "
         << setw(2)      << hour        << ":"
         << setw(2)      << min         << ":"
         << setw(2)      << sec         << " (GMT"
         << tz_sign      << offset/3600 << ")"
-        << setfill(' ') <<  endl;
+        << setfill(' ') << left        <<  endl;
 
    if (!copying)
       {
